<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The MSIX package manifest, templated the way `control.in` and the manual pages
  are. `build-msix.ps1` substitutes the placeholders below; nothing here is meant
  to be edited per build.

  ONE TEMPLATE, THREE PACKAGES

  odox ships three store packages rather than one, because a person opens a
  spreadsheet and a presentation through different doors and each door is its own
  listing. One template substituted three ways rather than three templates,
  because three files claiming three extensions in three near-identical ways is
  three chances for one of them to drift and no way to notice. `build-msix.ps1`
  holds the table of what each application substitutes, and the identities come
  from `identity.psd1`, which is not committed.

  The launcher is not here. It has no window, no file association and no store
  package, and it ships only on apt.

  WHAT MUST COME FROM PARTNER CENTER, AND WHY IT CANNOT BE GUESSED

  @IDENTITY_NAME@, @PUBLISHER@ and @PUBLISHER_DISPLAY_NAME@ are assigned when the
  name is reserved. A package whose identity disagrees with the reservation is
  rejected at upload rather than at review, so there is no way to find out you
  guessed wrong except by trying. @PUBLISHER@ in particular is a full X.500
  string, `CN=` with a GUID, and not the display name; it is also the subject a
  test-signing certificate must carry exactly, because `signtool` refuses to sign
  a package whose manifest publisher and whose certificate subject differ.

  @VERSION_APPX@ is what `packaging/version.sh` prints when asked for the appx
  spelling: four parts with the fourth always 0, which the Store requires. It is a different shape from the
  crate version and from what macOS wants, which is why one script answers all
  three.

  WHAT IS DECIDED HERE

  One file-type association per package, and the content types are not chosen
  here: they are OASIS's, they are what `odox_core::media_type` matches a package
  against, and they are the same strings the `.desktop` entries carry. A Windows
  install and a Linux one claim the same extensions under the same content types,
  so a person with both sees one application rather than two that disagree.

  A text document has a second media type, `...text-web`, which LibreOffice
  writes for a document converted from HTML. It is deliberately not registered:
  the extension is still `.odt`, an association is keyed by extension, and naming
  a second content type for the same extension gains nothing the reader does not
  already do by looking inside the package.

  `LSHandlerRank` has no Windows equivalent. An association here does not claim
  to be the default handler; it adds the application to `OpenWithProgids` and
  lets a person choose, which is the right posture for a format this suite reads
  and does not own.

  Each association carries a logo, which is the application's own drawing, and
  `make-icons` builds all three from the SVGs the Linux icons ship.

  No capabilities beyond `runFullTrust`. These applications make no network call,
  need no broad filesystem access - each opens the file it was launched with and
  writes nothing at all - and ask for no device. A capability requested and
  unused is a question at certification with no good answer, and it is what a
  person reads on the store page before installing.

  Author: David M. Anderson
  Built with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic)
-->
<Package
  xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/foundation/windows10"
  xmlns:uap="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/uap/windows10"
  xmlns:rescap="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/foundation/windows10/restrictedcapabilities"
  IgnorableNamespaces="uap rescap">

  <Identity
    Name="@IDENTITY_NAME@"
    Publisher="@PUBLISHER@"
    Version="@VERSION_APPX@"
    ProcessorArchitecture="x64" />

  <Properties>
    <DisplayName>@PRODUCT@</DisplayName>
    <PublisherDisplayName>@PUBLISHER_DISPLAY_NAME@</PublisherDisplayName>
    <Logo>Assets\StoreLogo.png</Logo>
    <Description>@DESCRIPTION@</Description>
  </Properties>

  <Dependencies>
    <!-- 10.0.17763 is the oldest Windows 10 that supports the packaging
         features used here. Raising it excludes machines for nothing; lowering
         it is not possible. -->
    <TargetDeviceFamily
      Name="Windows.Desktop"
      MinVersion="10.0.17763.0"
      MaxVersionTested="10.0.22621.0" />
  </Dependencies>

  <Resources>
    <!-- The package's languages. `en-us` because the applications are written in
         US English, `de-de` because they speak German. Both show in Partner
         Center's Packages panel, and both must match `build-msix.ps1`'s makepri
         `/dq`, which spells the pair `en-US_de-DE`, or the index has no default
         language and fails silently.

         Each is also a tag Partner Center opens a *new* submission's store
         listing under, which is how a package language becomes a listing
         language. It cannot rename a listing that already exists: that one is
         deleted and a new one added on the Store listings page. The fleet
         learned this on duckling and slipcase-desktop. -->
    <Resource Language="en-us" />
    <Resource Language="de-de" />
  </Resources>

  <Applications>
    <Application
      Id="@APPLICATION_ID@"
      Executable="@EXECUTABLE@"
      EntryPoint="Windows.FullTrustApplication">

      <uap:VisualElements
        DisplayName="@PRODUCT@"
        Description="@TILE_DESCRIPTION@"
        BackgroundColor="transparent"
        Square150x150Logo="Assets\Square150x150Logo.png"
        Square44x44Logo="Assets\Square44x44Logo.png">
        <uap:DefaultTile Wide310x150Logo="Assets\Wide310x150Logo.png" />
      </uap:VisualElements>

      <Extensions>
        <!-- One `uap:Extension` holding one `uap:FileTypeAssociation`: the
             schema allows only a single association child per extension, and a
             manifest with two is rejected by makeappx rather than by anything
             that would say why. The `Name` is the shell's key for the
             registration, lowercase with no dot, and it is what an upgrade
             matches on, so it is as fixed as the extension it stands for. -->
        <uap:Extension Category="windows.fileTypeAssociation">
          <uap:FileTypeAssociation Name="@TYPE_NAME@">
            <uap:SupportedFileTypes>
              <uap:FileType ContentType="@CONTENT_TYPE@">@EXTENSION@</uap:FileType>
            </uap:SupportedFileTypes>
            <uap:DisplayName>@TYPE_DISPLAY_NAME@</uap:DisplayName>
            <uap:Logo>Assets\FileTypeLogo.png</uap:Logo>
          </uap:FileTypeAssociation>
        </uap:Extension>
      </Extensions>
    </Application>
  </Applications>

  <Capabilities>
    <!-- A packaged desktop application needs this and nothing else. See the
         comment at the top of this file. -->
    <rescap:Capability Name="runFullTrust" />
  </Capabilities>
</Package>
